====================================================================================================
v117  SUBGROUP REGISTER:  d qubits instead of 2N
====================================================================================================
  A d-qubit register twirls over a SUBGROUP Q(B^T r). It separates the M
  terms iff B is injective on their symplectic vectors. Characters of a
  subgroup stay orthogonal, so the decode stays unbiased - which is why this
  is not forbidden by v107's failed random fractions.
  TIER C: structural, no circuit built.

    family            N    M    2N    log2M    d found   ctrl gates   qubits saved
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   crosstalk N=3        3     9     6       4         4           5           2
   crosstalk N=4        4    13     8       4         4          13           4
   crosstalk N=5        5    17    10       5         5          13           5
   crosstalk N=6        6    21    12       5         5          18           7
   crosstalk N=7        7    25    14       5         6          29           8
   crosstalk N=8        8    29    16       5         6          54          10

====================================================================================================
SCALING OF THE SAVING
====================================================================================================
     N     current 2N     subgroup d     ratio      ctrl gates: 2N -> sum|row|
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     3          6             4        1.50x            6 ->   5
     4          8             4        2.00x            8 ->  13
     5         10             5        2.00x           10 ->  13
     6         12             5        2.40x           12 ->  18
     7         14             6        2.33x           14 ->  29
     8         16             6        2.67x           16 ->  54

   d ~ N^0.471   (2N is N^1.0; log2 M ~ log N would be N^0)

====================================================================================================
WHAT THE REDUCTION DOES NOT FIX
====================================================================================================
  crosstalk N=4, full 2N=8 register vs subgroup d=4 register:
     degree-2 alias triples, full register     : 9
     degree-2 alias triples, subgroup register : 33

  WORSE, NOT IDENTICAL - and this refutes what the docstring of this file
  originally asserted. v_j + v_k = v_m does imply B v_j + B v_k = B v_m, so
  every existing alias survives. But B is injective only on the M points, not
  on all of F_2^{2N}, so it CREATES coincidences that were not there: fewer
  register bits, fewer sign patterns, more collisions among degree-2 products.
  The one-directional implication was mistaken for a biconditional.

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  THE QUBIT SAVING IS REAL. d sits at or one above the information bound
  ceil(log2 M), so the register is logarithmic in M where it was linear in N.
  The fitted d ~ N^0.47 above OVERSTATES the growth: the bound is 4,4,5,5,5,5
  and the search returned 4,4,5,5,6,6, missing the optimum at N=7,8. That is a
  search budget artefact, not scaling - more trials would flatten it.

  BUT IT IS BOUGHT TWICE OVER, and both costs land on axes that already hurt:

     controlled gates   2N -> sum|row|    16 -> 54 at N=8   (3.4x worse)
     alias triples       9 -> 33          at N=4            (3.7x worse)

  Gates matter because depth is where this construction is already weakest.
  Aliasing matters more, because it is ALREADY the accuracy ceiling on the
  weak coefficients (v106), and the reduction lowers that ceiling further.

  NET: a qubit saving paid for in the currency that was already scarce. The
  2N-ancilla objection was the strongest argument against this construction,
  but answering it by compressing the register makes the estimator worse at
  the thing it is already worst at. That is not obviously a good trade and
  this file does not claim it is one.

  A CHEAPER VARIANT WORTH TRYING: d strictly between log2 M and 2N. The
  extremes are 2N qubits / 9 aliases and log2 M qubits / 33 aliases; nothing
  here measures the middle, and the alias count should interpolate. If it
  falls off fast, a modest reduction may cost almost nothing.

  NOT ANSWERED HERE, and it is the tier-A question: does a d-qubit register
  reproduce the 2N-qubit accuracy at matched shots on real circuits? The
  orthogonality argument says the DECODE stays unbiased, and that argument
  is sound - but it says nothing about the alias inflation just measured,
  which is exactly the failure mode it does not model.
